MoBetta: winemac.drv: Keep mouse-look under a clip rect that holds the window.

Mouse-look (0006) stood aside whenever the cursor was clipped. WoW 3.3.5a
clips the cursor to its monitor when it goes fullscreen and does not release
it on going back to windowed, so from the first fullscreen on every camera
drag took the old path: a CGWarpMouseCursorPosition per frame and absolute
moves, ~50 warps/s against ~78/s before fullscreen in the same session, felt
as a chop while dragging (MoBetta Session #23, +cursor trace).

Mouse-look now also detaches while clipping with the confinement handler when
the clip rect holds the capture window's content rect: the detached cursor
stays frozen inside the window, so the clip still holds. The event-tap
handler warps the cursor itself and keeps the old rule.

--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_app.m
@@ -1107,8 +1107,28 @@
      * window on a display left of the main one, over other apps. */
     - (BOOL) shouldDetachForMouseLook
     {
-        return clientWantsCursorHidden && mouseCaptureWindow && !self.clippingCursor &&
+        return clientWantsCursorHidden && mouseCaptureWindow && [self cursorClippingAllowsMouseLook] &&
                ![windowsBeingDragged count] && [NSApp isActive];
+    }
+
+    /* MoBetta: a clip rect that holds the whole capture window is kept by
+     * mouse-look too, since the detached cursor stays frozen inside that
+     * window.  WoW 3.3.5a clips to its monitor in fullscreen and never
+     * releases it, which otherwise left mouse-look off for the rest of the
+     * session (the slow CGWarpMouseCursorPosition path on every frame).  The
+     * event-tap handler warps the cursor itself, so it keeps the old rule. */
+    - (BOOL) cursorClippingAllowsMouseLook
+    {
+        NSRect contentRect;
+
+        if (!self.clippingCursor)
+            return TRUE;
+        if ([clipCursorHandler respondsToSelector:@selector(setCursorPosition:)])
+            return FALSE;
+
+        contentRect = [mouseCaptureWindow contentRectForFrameRect:[mouseCaptureWindow frame]];
+        [self flipRect:&contentRect];
+        return CGRectContainsRect(clipCursorHandler.cursorClipRect, NSRectToCGRect(contentRect));
     }
 
     - (BOOL) startMouseLook
